WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.248
A measurable requirement names the observation
and the conditions under which it must hold.

00:00:05.248 --> 00:00:10.439
The search example gives a proportion and a
latency threshold, but still needs a workload,

00:00:10.439 --> 00:00:15.572
dataset and measurement window. Counting
registered users does not specify concurrency or

00:00:15.572 --> 00:00:16.610
request intensity.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:21.555
Define how failures, timeouts and warm-up
periods enter a measurement so that different

00:00:21.555 --> 00:00:23.317
tests answer the same question.

00:00:23.317 --> 00:00:29.007
Other qualities require equal care. Accuracy
needs a defined error measure; maintainability

00:00:29.007 --> 00:00:34.322
needs a relevant change task or evidence rather
than a vague hope. XML, Java or a SQL

00:00:34.322 --> 00:00:39.012
database may be design constraints, but they do
not by themselves establish

00:00:39.012 --> 00:00:40.075
interoperability.

00:00:40.317 --> 00:00:45.212
Distinguish the objective from a proposed
mechanism and choose a test that addresses the

00:00:45.212 --> 00:00:49.997
objective. Any additional numerical targets in a
teaching exercise should be marked as

00:00:49.997 --> 00:00:55.059
assumptions. Do twenty million registered users
imply twenty million simultaneous requests?

00:00:55.059 --> 00:01:00.255
No. Registration, activity, concurrency and
request rate measure different things and

00:01:00.255 --> 00:01:02.578
require separate workload assumptions.

00:01:02.578 --> 00:01:08.164
The C1, C2 and T1 example models producers
feeding a consumer on one CPU. Under the stated

00:01:08.164 --> 00:01:13.440
teaching assumptions, each stage operation has
equal cost and completed throughput is

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.288
limited by the smaller of combined production
and consumption.

00:01:17.288 --> 00:01:22.779
A CPU budget of twelve stage operations per
second must cover both producing and consuming

00:01:22.779 --> 00:01:27.293
work, so maximizing each component independently
is not a valid objective.

00:01:27.293 --> 00:01:32.116
With producer rates three and three and a
consumer rate six, completed throughput is six

00:01:32.116 --> 00:01:37.158
while the total CPU budget is twelve. Giving all
three components four operations per second

00:01:37.158 --> 00:01:41.708
consumes the same budget but only completes four
because consumption is slower than

00:01:41.708 --> 00:01:42.311
production.

00:01:42.311 --> 00:01:48.623
This is a deliberately simplified capacity
model. Real systems add scheduling, unequal
costs

00:01:48.623 --> 00:01:54.936
and queueing; one hundred percent utilization
leaves no headroom and should not be described

00:01:54.936 --> 00:02:00.632
as unlimited capacity. Why can rates 3,3,6 beat
rates 4,4,4 with the same CPU cost?

00:02:00.811 --> 00:02:07.536
Both consume twelve stage operations per second,
but throughput is min(C1+C2,T1): six in the

00:02:07.536 --> 00:02:10.021
first case and four in the second.
